WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.069
I would say there are two
 critical aspects to film noir.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:08.575
One is the visual language, 

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:10.176
and one is the story language.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.278
The visual language, like horror,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:15.382
it comes extremely influenced
 by German Expressionism.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:18.518
a cinema of chiaroscuro
of light and shadow.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.153
I grew up with film noir. 

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.654
You know, they were
people's movies. 

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.890
They weren't fancy Hollywood. 

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.395
They were tough,
 pessimistic, scary fantasies.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.864
It's ultimately a moral
decision, your honor.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:31.731
You have characters 

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:35.769
that have not and tried
 to get it through violence.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:39.572
They tried to break that barrier
 that society denies them.

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:42.475
That makes it
 a really interesting 

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.078
existential American tragedy.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.514
And of course, I loved that
 they were vital

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.349
and they were kind of B-movies. 

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:52.285
The commonality between
noir and tragedy

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:55.822
is the inexorable,
 you know it's going to end bad.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.792
That's very attractive

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.827
to see these
desperate stories. 

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.762
You could empathize
 with these people, 

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.764
but there was always
 a little bit of it,

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.833
“I understand, but that's not me.”

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.169
When people talk about noir,
people think immediately

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:11.438
of a cliché, a perfume ad.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.642
Venetian blinds, voice over,
 detective in that overcoat

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:19.712
in a wet street,
 slow-moving fan, smoking

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.347
dame in the half shadow. 

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.950
That's not noir. The figure
 of the femme fatale,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.252
they were women with agency. 

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.321
And they got it any way
 they could

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.590
because the game was rigged. 

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.927
This fate of the world and
themselves in the world,

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.895
that's very attractive
 when you're a young kid 

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.431
and you're trying to
figure out who you are. 

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:43.336
I actually like a lot
of lesser-known noirs.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:46.573
Born to Kill, Too Late for Tears

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:49.609
The brutal, inexorable
quality of noir

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:53.012
is that the wheels keep
grinding these characters.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.949
It’s a beautifully brutal genre.

